>Change the upper-case movement command from "move forever and attack anything
>in your way" into a new movement mode - "move aggressively into the neighbor-
>ing square, attacking anything in your way, jumping into any water that is
>there, etc."  The current upper-case movement definition is crazy anyway,
>anyone in their right mind should use <ctl>-movement, not <shft>-movement
>as currently implemented.

The problem with this is that on a PC with the number_pad option, there's
no such thing as <ctrl>-move.  I agree that SHIFT-move is far from
perfect, but I don't want to get rid of it.  And of course SHIFT-move
doesn't attack anything.

>So as long as you are using the lower-case movement commands you will
>never attack or jump into water (unless blind or confused or hallucinating).
><shft>-move is an aggressive move and *will* move you into water and will
>attack any neighboring creature.  <ctl>-move would act the same as it
>etc...

I agree with David and Richard.  The solution is not to improve the current
move commands (by confirmation or whatever), but to separate them from
the attack commands.  I think f-<dir> for fighting is a fine idea.  I
don't really see the need for a fight mode.  It also doesn't seem like that
much of a burden to make fighting a two keystroke command.  Since fighting
is a different thing than moving, it ought to be a different command.

I also like the idea of #swim for pools.  Swimming is different than moving.
The other day I almost drowned when I walked across water with a ring of 
levitation on, took off the ring to pick some stuff up, and then walked back
into the pool.  Considering that there's no indication when you're levitating,
which is something that would be pretty obvious if I were really levitating,
this doesn't seem like desirable behavior.


